answers.body:
The problem is that reduction(+:i) clause creates a local variable i , and you are supposed to change this local variable. In your code, however,  you increment the global one by calling function P , which is not thread safe (it has a race condition when incrementing it). So, you just have to make sure that you increment the local i when calling function P : void P(int& i){
       i++;
    }

    //in main:
    for (y = 0; y < yMax; y++)
            {P(i);} Another (but slower) option is to make function P threadsafe by using an atomic operation. In this case you do not need reduction at all: void P(){
#pragma omp atomic
 i++;
}

