There are two ways you may be able to improve your performance (no guarantees): Utilize SIMD, this now comes with OpenMP (check compiler availability - not all compilers offer SIMD). You could implement it like this: #pragma omp parallel for
for(int i = 0; i < A->dim.rows; i++) {
    for(int j = 0; j < B->dim.cols; j++) {
        C->data[i][j] = 0;
        #pragma omp simd
        for (int k = 0; k < A->dim.cols; k++) {
        C->data[i][j] += A->data[i][k] * B->data[k][j];
       }
   }
} Consider the impact of contiguous memory allocation and the impact it has on SIMD instructions and speed of access. Typically this makes SIMD operations "row fast" and "column slow". This may impact how you choose to allocate your memory. Note 1: Please be careful when you use #pragma omp parallel for please ensure that you utilize private variables (or the reduction clause) as appropriate to prevent race conditions: #pragma omp parallel for reduction(+:C->data[i][j]) Note 2: It is possible you should use the collapse clause this sometimes improves performance: #pragma omp parallel for collapse(3)

