I have issues with mcmapply that only occures if an Rcpp-function, testfun() , is first called outside mcmapply and then called through mcmapply . The issue only exists if: testfun() uses openMP mcmapply uses parallelization ( mc.cores >1) If I call mcmapply with testfun() , it runs and finishes without errors. If I first call testfun() separately and afterwards mcmapply , then mcmapply stalls. MWE: Rcpp::sourceCpp(code=
"  #include <RcppArmadillo.h>;
  // [[Rcpp::depends(RcppArmadillo)]]
  // [[Rcpp::export]]
  void testfun(int n) {
    arma::mat foo = arma::sqrt(arma::zeros(n,n));
  }")
library(parallel)

print("Run mcmapply+testfun: works")
mcmapply(mc.cores=2, function(x) testfun(100), 1:2)

print("Calling testfun separately: works")
testfun(100)

print("Run mcmapply+testfun after having run testfun separately: stalls")
mcmapply(mc.cores=2, function(x) testfun(100), 1:2) It as to do with the use of openMP and I suspect the issue has to do with storing the matrices, as it only occurs if testfun() assigns the result of arma::sqrt() to an arma::mat . The issue does not appear if I specify #define ARMA_DONT_USE_OPENMP . Setup: linux with R 4.4.0, gcc 14.2.0, and gsl 2.7.1, but issue also replicated on older R versions.

answers.body:
(Updated answer) It all works here if alter your example to actually compute something, and return a modified matrix. Rcpp::sourceCpp(code=
"  #include <RcppArmadillo.h>;
  // [[Rcpp::depends(RcppArmadillo)]]
  // [[Rcpp::export]]
  arma::mat testfun(int n) {
    arma::mat foo = arma::sqrt(arma::randu(n,n));
    return foo;
  }")
library(parallel)

print("Run mcmapply+testfun: works")
set.seed(123)
mcmapply(mc.cores=2, function(x) testfun(5), 1:2)

print("Calling testfun separately: works")
set.seed(123)
testfun(5)

print("Run mcmapply+testfun after having run testfun separately: stalls")
set.seed(123)
mcmapply(mc.cores=2, function(x) testfun(5), 1:2) Addendum: One reason for the stall you see could be that these *apply functions are not just launchers: they accumulate results. Yet your function did not return anything! Addendum Two: As seen in the comments discussion, this turned out to be mcmapply() and its forked child processes being unhappy with the (implicit) OpenMP use by Armadillo (for large enough matrices).  The trick then is to define ARMA_DONT_USE_OPENMP . (Initial answer) This violates the well-documented (see eg this post from a few days ago ) rule that you cannot call back from parallel sections into R, or touch R objects.  For convenience and default, RcppArmadillo vectors and matrices use the R memory, and this violates it.  See the RcppParallel vignette for discussion, and its RVector and RMatrix classes for examples.

