answers.body:
Generally speaking, necessary conditions for an efficient OpenMP parallelization are each thread has a significant amount of work to perform, otherwise the OpenMP overheads rule the runtime the "arithmetic intensity" (i.e. the number of operations per input/output byte) is high enough, otherwise the code is "memory bound" (i.e. the bottleneck is the CPU-RAM transfers and little speed-up can be obtained by using more cores) For the first condition I don't know how big is your n . For the second condition for each element of result your are performing only 4 assignements and 4 additions, which is a low arithmetic intensity. Anyway... As mentionned by Laci in the comments there is a race condition on cnt between different threads. Proctecting the increment instruction with an atomic clause is not not enough here, as it will be still incremented out of order. So the linear clause is the only simple solution. Also, you don't really need here to define a parallel region that encompasses the 4 loops, and each loop can define its own parallel region: cnt = 1;
    for ( int layer = 0; layer < (n + 1) / 2; layer++) {
        // direction 1 - traverse from left to right
        #pragma omp parallel for linear(cnt:1)
